    <body>
        <segment id="1" parent="15" relname="span">When you get a chance, find this article in the ACM Digital Library:</segment>
        <segment id="2" parent="1" relname="enablement">Chrusch, M. (2000, September &amp; October). Seven great myths of usability. Interactions, 13-16.</segment>
        <segment id="3" parent="20" relname="span">In this article Marc Chrusch brings up some very interesting areas to consider that are actually myths about usability.</segment>
        <segment id="4" parent="16" relname="span">These myths are based on mis-education and also misuse of terms.</segment>
        <segment id="5" parent="19" relname="span">Here are the seven myths.</segment>
        <segment id="6" parent="5" relname="elaboration">Go read the article for more information:</segment>
        <segment id="7" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #1:  Usability only increases development costs and lengthens development time.</segment>
        <segment id="8" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #2:  The user interface is really just adding good practices to make the application appealing</segment>
        <segment id="9" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #3:  Usability is user interface design.</segment>
        <segment id="10" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #4:  Usability is really just common sense.</segment>
        <segment id="11" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #5:  As long as developers are familar with guidelines, good user interfaces will be designed.</segment>
        <segment id="12" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #6:  Usability testing is not needed if the development team has been working with the users a long time and knows what they want.</segment>
        <segment id="13" parent="18" relname="list">Myth #7:  "We'll handle that in the help/training/documentation."</segment>
        <group id="14" type="span"/>
        <group id="15" type="span" parent="14" relname="span"/>
        <group id="16" type="span" parent="3" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="17" type="span" parent="4" relname="elaboration"/>
        <group id="18" type="multinuc" parent="17" relname="span"/>
        <group id="19" type="span" parent="18" relname="preparation"/>
        <group id="20" type="span" parent="15" relname="motivation"/>
    </body>
